# What a second run of each workload costs, and which of them are held to
# costing nothing.
#
# tests/check-vm-growth.sh runs every workload in this directory twice in one
# interpreter and asks what the second run cost. A price paid once -- a name
# interned for the first time, a face opened, a table sized on demand -- is
# paid by the first run and not the second. Anything the second run pays for
# again is paid by every job after it too, which is the shape of the only
# leak neither the sanitizers nor the collector can see: memory that is
# allocated, still named, and named forever.
#
# A single run's figure is a sample, not a verdict: its sign moves with the
# host's allocator state, down to the length of the data directory's path.
# So a workload whose second run costs anything is judged over a settled
# window -- five runs to let the free list cover the sizes asked for, then
# three measured runs -- and it is holding only where every one of the
# three costs something. The smallest of the three is the settled figure,
# and it is what a retention cannot get under.
#
# The classes here are MEASURED, not chosen, and the guard re-derives every
# one of them on every run:
#
#   zero          Runs cleanly twice and the second run costs nothing. Held,
#                 with fits, to reaching nothing within the settled window
#                 -- no tolerance, and no assumption about how wide an
#                 object is, which is what makes it hold on every build.
#                 Which of the two names a settling workload wears records
#                 what the deriving host's allocator happened to cover by
#                 run two, so the two are one assertion.
#
#   fits          Costs something on a second run and nothing within the
#                 settled window.
#                 Not a leak, and not the collector's doing: what the
#                 previous run released has been given back -- five
#                 collections between runs instead of one, over both banks
#                 instead of one, give the same figure to the byte -- but the
#                 allocator serves a request out of a released block only
#                 where that block is at least as large as the request: the
#                 admission test is a plain comparison of the two sizes,
#                 best-fit upward from the request's own size class. A run
#                 asking for a spread of sizes therefore needs fresh space
#                 for every size larger than anything released so far.
#                 Measured directly: a body whose requests are all one size,
#                 or descend in size, costs NOTHING from its second run,
#                 while the same body with ascending sizes costs 69,480 then
#                 33,096 then 19,056. Thirty-four of the forty-nine that
#                 cost anything on a second run are this and nothing more.
#                 The assertion is that they still reach nothing.
#
#   costs <why>   Costs something on every run of the settled window: even
#                 the smallest measured run is positive -- of three runs
#                 where this register already carries the workload here, of
#                 eleven where convicting it would be news. That
#                 may be a retention, or the fit effect needing more passes
#                 than the window holds; which it is has not been
#                 established per workload. These are the worklist.
#                 Reported if it becomes free, so it can be promoted.
#
#   once <why>    Does not survive being run twice in one interpreter: it
#                 makes something inaccessible, leaves a device installed,
#                 or otherwise depends on being the first thing to run. A
#                 workload that fails to run allocates nothing and would
#                 read as the cleanest of all, so this class is refused a
#                 measurement rather than credited with a good one.
#                 Reported if it starts surviving, so it can be promoted.
#
# A class written as two names separated by a slash states one per
# composite-size build, narrow/wide, for the workloads whose behaviour
# genuinely differs between the two; the guard asks the interpreter which
# build it is and judges that half.
#
# WHAT `unexplained` MEANS, and it is not a shrug. The class is a
# measurement and is true. The CAUSE, for a workload marked unexplained, has
# not been established -- it is an open worklist, and the guard prints how
# many are left so it stays visible rather than settling in. Nothing here is
# a guess dressed as a reason: where a cause is written down it was measured.
#
#   <workload>  <class>  <why, for costs and once>
